{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said: the outcry, etc.</b> The reason He said <b>ki</b> ('because'), and did not suffice to say 'the outcry of Sodom is great, etc., and their sin is heavy' — also He doubled 'outcry' and 'their sin,' yet an outcry is only because of sin. Also His saying <b>very heavy</b> — He intended to say that at that time all the nations too were provocative in their deeds, but these excelled in wickedness; therefore He said <b>ki rabbah</b> ('because it is great'), meaning: the reason for My strictness is not because theirs alone was the outcry that ascended before Me, but because their outcry was greater than all the nations. And He doubled 'their outcry' and 'their sin' corresponding to two wickednesses: the first, that they were evil toward their fellow creatures, as our Sages said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 109a), and as in the incident of the dispute; and the second, that they were evil toward Heaven, as their deeds revealed when they pursued the lie of male mating with the angels that appeared in the likeness of men — and HaShem needed to make known the extremity of their wickedness, they being the work of His hands." "<b>Further,</b> HaShem intended to dispel the cold of His anxiety — for one might say that He exerted Himself to save a nation so wicked. Therefore He said to him <b>ki rabbah ki khavdah</b> ('because it is great, because it is heavy'), etc., meaning: the wickedness for which they are liable was of recent origin, and previously they were not so wicked as when you saved them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:11.356034683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}